WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.420
What makes a city? People? Buildings?

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.340
Horrific traffic jams? That's not too far off.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.600
It turns out that roads are

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.660
what connect all the parts of a city.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.840
We need easy paths to travel from place to place

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.960
and for moving materials, supplies, and services.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.279
And in some parts of cities—slums— 
that's not happening.

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:26.702
The world's slums lack not only large, easy to travel roads,

00:00:26.702 --> 00:00:29.533
but they're also missing a lot of the infrastructure

00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:30.984
that goes along with them:

00:00:30.984 --> 00:00:35.820
street signs, building numbers, and vehicle access to
building exteriors.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.460
That lack of connectivity means that the billion
people living in slums can't reliably

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.160
get their mail, get to work, or even get
to the hospital in an emergency.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.100
Trash removal and sanitation are difficult at best.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.820
Now, scientists are trying to transform slums with

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.960
a mathematical approach to urban design called "topology".

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.714
Slums can be broken down into two
components:

00:00:59.714 --> 00:01:02.039
infrastructure, like homes and stores,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.280
and the access network that links
them.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.920
Nodes and connections.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.448
With this framework, connections --in effect access--

00:01:12.448 --> 00:01:13.900
can be optimized.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:19.350
This new approaches already been used in two slums within Cape Town, South Africa.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.374
In one of the two neighborhoods,

00:01:21.374 --> 00:01:24.220
most essential services provided by the city—

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.340
water, toilets, electricity, trash removal—

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.820
are on the outskirts where road access exists,

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.500
but the inner portions of the
settlement—

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.500
where temples are—are completely cut off.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.440
In this study scientists used satellite data and

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.280
detailed maps handmade by locals to diagram

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.680
all the nodes and connections of the settlement.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.790
They then used their topological algorithm to figure out

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.965
which areas were inaccessible by roads,

00:01:50.965 --> 00:01:54.740
then generated the most cost effective 
 ways to build roads

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.500
and connect them to
outside services.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.700
In topology since one block of buildings

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.980
surrounded by roads is considered equivalent,

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.597
settlements with a wide range of shapes and sizes,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.680
from the suburbs of Las Vegas to the slums of Mumbai,

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.040
can be analyzed in the same way.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.320
The scientists who designed the program

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.280
hope to come up with a user-friendly interface that

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.580
can be used by communities around the world.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:22.920
That, they say, would put the decision-making power

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.795
into the hands of locals who can determine if proposed

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.920
access roads will accommodate their priorities,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.960
then reroute them accordingly.

